== Chapter 7

#align(center)[#smallcaps(all: true)[THE TESTIMONY OF THE SPIRIT NECESSARY TO CONFIRM SCRIPTURE, SO THAT ITS AUTHORITY MAY BE CERTAIN. THE IMPIETY OF CLAIMING THAT THE CREDIBILITY OF SCRIPTURE DEPENDS ON THE JUDGMENT OF THE CHURCH.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. The authority of Scripture derived not from men, but from the Spirit of God. Objection: that Scripture depends on the decision of the Church. Refutation: I. The truth of God would thus be made subject to the will of man. II. It is an insult to the Holy Spirit. III. It sets up a tyranny in the Church. IV. It produces a mass of errors. V. It overturns conscience. VI. It exposes our faith to the scoffs of the ungodly.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. Another reply to the objection, drawn from the words of the Apostle Paul. The difficulties raised by opponents resolved. A second objection refuted.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. A third objection, founded on a saying of Augustine, considered.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. Conclusion: that the authority of Scripture is founded on its being spoken by God. This confirmed by the conscience of the godly, and the agreement of all men with the least candor. A fourth objection, common in the mouths of unbelievers. Refutation.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[5\. Last and necessary conclusion: that the authority of Scripture is sealed on the hearts of believers by the testimony of the Holy Spirit. The certainty of this testimony. Confirmation of it from a passage of Isaiah and the experience of believers. Also from another passage of Isaiah.]

#v(0.8em)

1\. #smallcaps[Before] going further, it seems proper to make some observations on the authority of Scripture, so that our minds may not only be prepared to receive it with reverence, but be freed of all doubt.

When what is set forth as the Word of God is acknowledged to be so, no one, unless he is without common sense and the feelings of a man, will have the desperate audacity to refuse belief to the speaker. But no oracles are given from heaven every day, and the Scriptures are the only records in which God has been pleased to commit his truth to lasting remembrance. So the full authority which they ought to have with the faithful is not recognized unless they are believed to have come from heaven, as directly as if God had been heard speaking them. This subject well deserves to be treated at greater length and weighed more carefully. But my readers will pardon me for paying more regard to what my plan allows than to what the breadth of this topic requires.

A most harmful error has very widely prevailed, namely, that Scripture has only as much weight as the approval of the Church grants it; as if the eternal and inviolable truth of God could depend on the will of men. With great insult to the Holy Spirit, they ask: Who can assure us that the Scriptures came from God? Who can guarantee that they have come down safe and unimpaired to our times? Who can persuade us that #emph[this] book is to be received with reverence, and #emph[that one] struck from the list, unless the Church settled all these things with certainty? So, they say, both the reverence due to Scripture and the books to be admitted into the canon depend on the decision of the Church. In this way sacrilegious men, seeking to bring in unbridled tyranny under the pretext of the Church, do not care in what absurdities they entangle themselves and others, as long as they extort from the simple this one admission, namely, that there is nothing the Church cannot do. But what is to become of miserable consciences seeking some solid assurance of eternal life, if all the promises about it have no better support than man’s judgment? On being told so, will they stop doubting and trembling? On the other hand, to what jeers of the wicked is our faith exposed, and into how great suspicion is it brought with everyone, if it is believed to have only a precarious authority lent to it by the good will of men?

2\. These ravings are admirably refuted by a single word of an apostle. Paul testifies that the Church is “built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets,” (Eph 2:20). If the doctrine of the apostles and prophets is the foundation of the Church, the former must have had its certainty before the latter began to exist. Nor is there any room for the quibble that, though the Church takes her first beginning from there, it still remains doubtful what writings are to be attributed to the apostles and prophets until her judgment steps in. For if the Christian Church was founded at first on the writings of the prophets and the preaching of the apostles, that doctrine, wherever it may be found, was certainly established and approved before the Church, since without it the Church herself could never have existed.#footnote[The French adds, “Comme le fondement va deuant l’edifice;”—as the foundation goes before the house. (Beveridge’s note.)] Nothing, therefore, can be more absurd than the fiction that the power of judging Scripture lies with the Church, and that its certainty depends on her nod. When the Church receives it and gives it the stamp of her authority, she does not make authentic what was otherwise doubtful or disputed. Rather, acknowledging it as the truth of God, she shows her reverence, as duty requires, by an unhesitating assent. As for their question, how we shall be persuaded that it came from God without turning to a decree of the Church, it is just as if someone asked how we shall learn to distinguish light from darkness, white from black, sweet from bitter. Scripture bears on its face as clear evidence of its truth as white and black do of their color, and sweet and bitter of their taste.

3\. I know it is common to quote a saying of Augustine in which he says that he would not believe the gospel if he were not moved by the authority of the Church (Aug. Cont. Epist. Fundament. c. 5). But it is easy to see from the context how inaccurate and unfair it is to give it such a meaning. He was arguing against the Manichees, who insisted on being believed without question, claiming that they had the truth, though they did not show that they had it. But as they made a show of appealing to the gospel in support of Manes, he asks what they would do if they came across a man who did not even believe the gospel—what kind of argument they would use to bring him over to their opinion. He then adds, “But I would not believe the gospel,” etc.; meaning that when he was a stranger to the faith, the only thing that could bring him to embrace the gospel as the certain truth of God was the authority of the Church. And is it any wonder that one who does not know Christ should pay regard to men?

Augustine, therefore, does not say here that the faith of the godly is founded on the authority of the Church; nor does he mean that the certainty of the gospel depends on it. He merely says that unbelievers would have no certainty of the gospel, by which they might be won to Christ, unless they were moved by the consent of the Church. And he clearly shows this to be his meaning by saying a little before: “When I have praised my own creed and ridiculed yours, what do you suppose we ought to judge, or what more is to be done, than to leave those who invite us to certainty and afterwards command us to believe what is uncertain, and to follow those who invite us first to believe what we are not yet able to comprehend, so that, growing stronger through faith itself, we may become able to understand what we believe—no longer men, but God himself inwardly strengthening and enlightening our minds?” These are unquestionably the words of Augustine (August. Cont. Epist. Fundament. cap. 14); and the obvious inference from them is that this holy man had no intention of making our faith in Scripture hang on the nod or decision of the Church,#footnote[The French adds, “La destournant du seul fondement qu’elle a en l’Escriture;”—turning it away from the only foundation which it has in Scripture. (Beveridge’s note.)] but only of indicating (what we too admit to be true) that those who are not yet enlightened by the Spirit of God are made teachable by reverence for the Church, and so submit to learn the faith of Christ from the gospel. In this way, though the authority of the Church leads us on and prepares us to believe the gospel, it is plain that Augustine would have the certainty of the godly rest on a very different foundation.#footnote[Augustin. De Ordine, lib. 2 c. 9 “Ad discendum dupliciter movemur, auctoritate atque ratione: tempore auctoritas, re autem ratio prior est,” etc. \[We are moved to learn in two ways, by authority and by reason: in time authority comes first, but in the thing itself reason.\] “Itaque quamquam bonorum auctoritas imperitae multitudini videatur esse salubrior, ratio vero aptior eruditis: tamen quia nullus hominum nisi ex imperito peritus fit, &c., evenit ut omnibus bona, magna, occulta discere cupientibus, non aperiat nisi auctoritas januam,” etc. \[And so, although the authority of good men may seem more wholesome for the unlearned multitude, and reason better suited to the educated, yet, since no man becomes learned except from being unlearned, etc., it comes about that for all who desire to learn things good, great and hidden, only authority opens the door.\] He has many other excellent things to the same effect. (Beveridge’s note.)]

At the same time, I do not deny that he often presses the Manichees with the consent of the whole Church, when arguing in support of the Scriptures, which they rejected. So he reproaches Faustus (lib. 32) for not submitting to the truth of the gospel—truth so well founded, so firmly established, so gloriously renowned, and handed down by sure succession from the days of the apostles. But he nowhere suggests that the authority which we give to the Scriptures depends on the definitions or decrees of men. He only brings forward the universal judgment of the Church as a point most relevant to the case, and one, moreover, in which he had the advantage over his opponents. Anyone who wants to see this more fully proved may read his short treatise, #emph[De Utilitate Credendi] (The Advantages of Believing). There he will find that the only readiness to believe which he recommends is one that opens the way and makes a fitting start to inquiry; while he declares that we ought not to be satisfied with opinion, but to rest on certain and solid truth.

4\. We must hold to what I said a little earlier, that our faith in doctrine is not established until we are fully convinced that God is its author. So the highest proof of Scripture is uniformly drawn from the character of him whose Word it is. The prophets and apostles do not boast of their own sharpness of mind or of any qualities that win credit for speakers, nor do they dwell on reasons; but they appeal to the sacred name of God, so that the whole world may be compelled to submit. The next thing to consider is how it appears, not merely probable but certain, that the name of God is put forward neither rashly nor deceitfully. If, then, we want to provide as well as possible for our consciences, and save them from being driven about in a whirl of uncertainty, from wavering, and even from stumbling at the smallest obstacle, our conviction of the truth of Scripture must come from a higher source than human conjectures, judgments, or reasons; namely, the secret testimony of the Spirit. It is true that, if we choose to proceed by argument, it is easy to establish, by evidence of various kinds, that if there is a God in heaven, the Law, the Prophecies, and the Gospel came from him. Indeed, even if learned men, and men of the greatest ability, should take the opposite side, summoning and showing off all the powers of their minds in the debate, unless they are lost to all shame they will be compelled to confess that Scripture shows clear evidence of being spoken by God, and so of containing his heavenly doctrine. We shall see a little further on that the volume of sacred Scripture far surpasses all other writings. Indeed, if we look at it with clear eyes and unbiased judgment, it will at once present itself with a divine majesty that will subdue our presumptuous opposition and force us to do it homage.

Still, it is getting things backward to try to build up a full faith in Scripture by argument. True, if I were called to contend with the craftiest despisers of God, who want to be thought shrewd and witty in undermining Scripture, I trust that, though I do not have the highest ability or eloquence, I should not find it hard to stop their noisy mouths. If anything were to be gained by refuting their quibbles, I could without much trouble put down the boasts that they mutter in corners. But although we may defend the sacred Word of God against those who speak against it, it does not follow that we shall at once implant in their hearts the certainty which faith requires. Irreligious men think that religion rests only on opinion; and so, that they may not believe foolishly or on slight grounds, they desire and insist that it be proved by reason that Moses and the prophets were divinely inspired. But I answer that the testimony of the Spirit is superior to reason. For as God alone is a sufficient witness of himself in his own words, so these words will not gain full credit in the hearts of men until they are sealed by the inward testimony of the Spirit. The same Spirit, therefore, who spoke by the mouth of the prophets, must penetrate our hearts, to persuade us that they faithfully delivered the message divinely entrusted to them. This connection is most aptly expressed by Isaiah in these words: “My Spirit, who is on you, and My words that I have put in your mouth, will not depart from your mouth or from the mouths of your children and grandchildren, from now on and forevermore, says the #smallcaps[Lord],” (Isa 59:21). Some good people are troubled because, while the wicked murmur against the Word of God unpunished, they have no clear proof at hand to silence them. They forget that the Spirit is called a pledge and seal to confirm the faith of the godly for this very reason: until he enlightens their minds, they are tossed to and fro in a sea of doubts.

5\. Let it therefore be held as fixed that those who are inwardly taught by the Holy Spirit rest firmly on Scripture; that Scripture, carrying its own evidence with it, does not stoop to be subjected to proofs and arguments, but owes the full conviction with which we ought to receive it to the testimony of the Spirit. For though of itself it wins reverence by its own majesty, yet it takes serious hold of us only when it is sealed on our hearts by the Spirit. Enlightened by him, we no longer believe, on our own judgment or on that of others, that the Scriptures are from God; but, in a way above human judgment, we are perfectly assured—as much as if we there beheld the Godhead of God himself—that it came to us, through the ministry of men, from the very mouth of God. We do not ask for proofs or probabilities on which to rest our judgment, but we submit our intellect and judgment to it as something too transcendent for us to estimate. We do this, however, not in the way some people seize on something unknown, which displeases them as soon as it is known, but because we are thoroughly convinced that in holding it we hold unassailable truth; not like miserable men whose minds are enslaved by superstition, but because we feel a divine energy living and breathing in it—an energy by which we are drawn and kindled to obey it, willingly indeed and knowingly, but more vividly and effectually than human will or knowledge could bring about. So God most justly cries out by the mouth of Isaiah that the prophets, with the whole people, are his witnesses (Isa 43:10); because, being taught by prophecies, they held without doubt that God had spoken without deceit or ambiguity.

Such, then, is a conviction that asks for no reasons; such is a knowledge that agrees with the highest reason, namely, a knowledge in which the mind rests more firmly and securely than in any reasons; such, in short, is the conviction that revelation from heaven alone can produce. I say nothing more than every believer experiences in himself, though my words fall far short of the reality. I do not dwell on this subject now, because we will return to it again. Only let us now understand that the only true faith is that which the Spirit of God seals on our hearts. Indeed, the modest and teachable reader will find a sufficient reason in the promise in Isaiah that all the children of the renewed Church “will be taught by the #smallcaps[Lord],” (Isa 54:13). God grants this special privilege to his elect only, whom he separates from the rest of mankind. For what is the beginning of true doctrine but a ready eagerness to hear the Word of God? And God, by the mouth of Moses, demands to be heard in this way: “Do not say in your heart, Who will ascend into heaven? or, Who will descend into the deep? Behold, the word is in your mouth,” (Deut 30:12, 14). Since God has been pleased to reserve the treasure of understanding for his children, it is no wonder that so much ignorance and stupidity is seen in the #emph[mass] of mankind. In the mass I include even the most eminent, until they are grafted into the body of the Church. Isaiah, moreover, warning that the prophetic doctrine would prove incredible not only to strangers but also to the Jews, who wanted to be thought members of the household of God, adds the reason when he asks, “To whom has the arm of the #smallcaps[Lord] been revealed?” (Isa 53:1). If at any time, then, we are troubled by the small number of those who believe, let us on the other hand remember that none grasp the mysteries of God except those to whom it is given.
